  "preamble": "Glottalization: plain [t] becomes the ejective [tʼ]. In Ossetic, inherited tʼ is rare; the clearest case is the cluster *st in the word for 'star' (Proto-Iranian *Hstā́ryah > Iron стъалы, Digor ӕстъалу). Much Ossetic tʼ is in Kartvelian loans that already had it. Georgian renders Russian t as tʼ in loans (ტელეფონი 'telephone'). Ge'ez and the modern Ethiopian Semitic languages often render Greek t as the ejective ṭ [tʼ], though not always (Αἰθιοπία > ʾItyop̣p̣ya keeps plain t). Eastern Armenian dialects reportedly realise the Classical tenuis t as [tʼ]; the standard language does not. The PIE glottalic theory is a reconstruction hypothesis, not counted here.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Glottalization, Fortition, Loanword Adaptation",
